00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.560
base was made with the best possible intentions.

19
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.880
Nobody wants the code to get like this,

20
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.880
and it's sometimes referred to as the ball of mud pattern.

21
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.960
I don't think of brownfield code as a ball of mud.

22
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.600
I think of them much more like a city.

23
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.560
A city is

24
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.640
very successful, and it's something that's evolved over time.

25
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.320
And brownfield systems tend to work like this.

26
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.800
They work incredibly well.

27
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.160
They have huge numbers of customers working on them,

28
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.200
and that is why they've been around for five, ten, 15 years

29
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.320
while maintaining complex user demands, while at the same time working

30
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.080
through problems like performance, security and scaling that come with that.

31
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.640
And like the city, they've evolved as a response to the needs

32
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.920
and there are areas in that functioning city.

33
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.080
So this is actually London.

34
