I took this last weekend from one of the top of the skyscrapers.

35
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.880
I'm very pleased with this picture.

36
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.640
It's lovely, but you can see on this you've got paths through the city

37
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.960
which are very, very clear.

38
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.480
These are

39
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.080
these are the well-trodden paths, the ones that we all know where it's going.

40
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.200
And then there are less well maintained spaces, dead ends

41
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.360
and the places that you really wouldn't want to get caught in alone late at night.

42
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.120
So this is like your code base, and you want to evolve it into something

43
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.600
that is easier to build on, that's more understood,

44
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.120
and you get that high level view so you can start working through.

45
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.640
We also heard from Simon Martinelli, who does modernization

46
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.640
for a living and is blunt about what it isn't.

47
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.760
Translating COBOL into Java is not modernization.

48
00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.080
That is lift and shift.

49
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.760
What he does instead is reverse engineer the specification back out of the code,

50
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.800
get the business to check it, and generate from there.

51
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:37.120
that.

52
00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.800
What I do mostly is software modernization.

53
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.000
So I'm doing modernization for enterprise applications for about eight years now.

54
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.560
And there I just changed the process.

55
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.680
That means I extract

56
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.920
use case and entity model from code and tests and documentation.

57
00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.520
Maybe I have confidence here or whatever, because usually the documentation

58
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.800
is spread around multiple artifacts that we have,

59
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.080
and then we have the entity model and the use cases.

60
00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.600
They will be revised or reviewed by the business people.

61
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.320
And then we generate the new code, because there are a lot of IDs

62
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.080
that we can directly transform, maybe from COBOL to travel.

63
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.600
So that's also something that anthropic is, is telling us.

64
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.080
But that never worked.

65
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.000
So we did that like 30 years ago COBOL to see or call to C++ or something.

66
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.400
But that's not helpful because that's just lift and shift.

67
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.440
And modernization is not lift and shift.

68
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.840
Modernization is rethinking how people are working

69
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.640
with the software, integrating features that maybe are not there.

70
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.920
And because of this reverse engineering, we also have

71
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.240
a positive feedback from the end users because we are not transforming

72
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:59.120
from one technology to another, because regarding our use case and entity model.

73
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.800
And we can integrate new features because usually you don't do that.

74
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.240
So the guideline when we started with the modernization project

75
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.120
two years ago was we want to have the exact same system

76
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.400
just in another technology,

77
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.280
and you don't add new features because we don't want to introduce new bugs.

78
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.520
And now we can just do that because we just change the specification.

79
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.240
Christopher Batey has a warning

80
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.440
about something you've just inherited without noticing your harness,

81
00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.600
your model hosting, and your model are three boxes you don't control.

82
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.920
And if your provider goes down at the same moment as your production

83
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.920
outage, and that model is the only way your engineers know how to debug.

84
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.480
Good luck.

85
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.200
The next thing I wanted to dig a bit deeper into is have a real

86
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.280
think about the dependency you want to put on the producer of your digital product.

87
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.440
So if we go back to this diagram and we've accepted that,

